1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,072
This is a re-creation of a real job interview.

2
00:00:03,072 --> 00:00:06,144
The interviewer is an AI running on my laptop.

3
00:00:06,144 --> 00:00:08,081
No cloud models, no API keys.

4
00:00:08,081 --> 00:00:19,969
It joined a Google Meet, introduced itself, asked questions drawn from the candidate's resume and the job posting, listened, asked follow-ups, and afterwards wrote an assessment.

5
00:00:19,969 --> 00:00:22,708
A person still makes the hiring decision.

6
00:00:22,708 --> 00:00:26,114
Here is how it works, and what broke along the way.

7
00:00:26,614 --> 00:00:28,139
Senjin has two halves.

8
00:00:28,139 --> 00:00:34,723
Before the call, it plans: it reads the resume and the job description and decides what to ask.

9
00:00:34,723 --> 00:00:45,536
During the call, it listens with whisper dot c p p, thinks with a local Ollama model, and speaks with Piper, through virtual audio devices into Google Meet.

10
00:00:45,536 --> 00:00:49,626
After the call, a bigger local model scores the transcript.

11
00:00:49,626 --> 00:00:51,428
All of it runs on one Mac.

12
00:00:51,928 --> 00:00:53,255
The plan comes first.

13
00:00:53,255 --> 00:01:04,629
One command reads the job description, picks an interview shape, here a senior full stack profile with three background questions and five technical ones, and writes the questions.

14
00:01:04,629 --> 00:01:08,989
Half come from a shared question bank, half are written for this job.

15
00:01:08,989 --> 00:01:16,382
Then it prints a coverage report: which of the job's requirements the questions actually reach, and which they don't.

16
00:01:16,382 --> 00:01:21,942
For this posting, five of thirteen must-haves are covered by the default five questions.

17
00:01:21,942 --> 00:01:27,882
That gap is visible before anyone is interviewed, and you can edit the questions file by hand.

18
00:01:27,882 --> 00:01:33,316
The candidate's name and contact details are removed before any model sees the resume.

19
00:01:33,816 --> 00:01:37,865
Here is what happens inside the call, one reply at a time.

20
00:01:38,165 --> 00:01:43,702
The candidate speaks, and the call's audio reaches Senjin through a virtual audio device.

21
00:01:44,002 --> 00:01:52,538
A voice detector waits for them to stop, about a second of quiet, and holds a little longer after a short answer in case they carry on.

22
00:01:52,838 --> 00:01:56,774
Whisper turns the speech into text, in well under a second.

23
00:01:57,074 --> 00:02:03,339
Then the code, not the model, decides what happens next: a follow-up, the next question, or the end.

24
00:02:03,639 --> 00:02:07,360
The local model only puts that into natural words.

25
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:11,971
Piper speaks the reply back into the call, a sentence at a time.

26
00:02:12,271 --> 00:02:19,047
If a reply is slow, a short I see fills the gap. If the candidate cuts in, Senjin stops talking.

27
00:02:19,347 --> 00:02:25,848
In the last live call, that whole loop took about two seconds, from the end of speech to the first sound.

28
00:02:26,348 --> 00:02:30,605
The key design choice: the code runs the interview, not the model.

29
00:02:30,605 --> 00:02:35,120
Code picks the next question and decides when to follow up or move on.

30
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:36,797
The model only phrases it.

31
00:02:36,797 --> 00:02:46,214
That keeps every candidate on the same plan, keeps the questions from being invented, and lets me test the flow with fakes instead of real models.

32
00:02:46,214 --> 00:02:53,051
The interviewer is also told never to praise or criticize, and a filter removes praise if the model slips.

33
00:02:53,551 --> 00:02:58,693
After the call, the judge writes strengths, weaknesses and a score for each question.

34
00:02:58,693 --> 00:03:05,045
Every finding has to quote the candidate, and the code checks that the quote is really in the transcript.

35
00:03:05,045 --> 00:03:07,283
A finding it cannot prove is dropped.

36
00:03:07,283 --> 00:03:09,219
Then a fixed hiring bar applies.

37
00:03:09,219 --> 00:03:19,200
In the real call, the judge said Hire, the bar said technical six is below seven, and the report says so in plain words: No Hire, low confidence, and why it changed.

38
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:20,108
It is advisory.

39
00:03:20,108 --> 00:03:22,225
Someone has to read the transcript.

40
00:03:22,725 --> 00:03:24,197
I checked three things.

41
00:03:24,197 --> 00:03:29,444
Same transcript in, same report out: with temperature zero there was no variation.

42
00:03:29,444 --> 00:03:36,866
Then a fairness check: the same answers with different names and cues attached, with and without the redaction step.

43
00:03:36,866 --> 00:03:43,329
And speed: in one live call it took about two seconds from the candidate stopping to the first sound.

44
00:03:43,329 --> 00:03:45,376
The tests do not call any model.

45
00:03:45,376 --> 00:03:47,744
They use fakes, so they run anywhere.

46
00:03:48,244 --> 00:03:49,046
Plenty broke.

47
00:03:49,046 --> 00:03:53,367
A follow-up was lost when the candidate cut in before the model spoke.

48
00:03:53,367 --> 00:03:57,132
Praise leaked through when the model used a curly apostrophe.

49
00:03:57,132 --> 00:04:02,441
A quote check failed because the judge wrote a period where the candidate had a comma.

50
00:04:02,441 --> 00:04:07,132
The coverage report called questions a gap when they were only cut for time.

51
00:04:07,132 --> 00:04:12,934
And my own prompt change made the greeting drop the notice, which is why it is fixed text now.

52
00:04:12,934 --> 00:04:17,687
I also tried a small decision model to detect when the candidate is finished.

53
00:04:17,687 --> 00:04:22,563
It was more accurate on my small test set, but slower, so it is off by default.

54
00:04:23,063 --> 00:04:26,405
Four ideas are written down but not on the roadmap.

55
00:04:26,405 --> 00:04:28,305
First, small decision models.

56
00:04:28,305 --> 00:04:34,922
Ollama serves tiny models that answer questions like, has the candidate finished, with a probability.

57
00:04:34,922 --> 00:04:40,033
I wired one in as a second opinion on when the candidate's questions are over.

58
00:04:40,033 --> 00:04:51,171
On seventy hand-labelled turns it beat my rules on that decision, forty-three right to forty, but it added about six hundred milliseconds a call, so it is off by default.

59
00:04:51,171 --> 00:04:52,416
Second, scheduling.

60
00:04:52,416 --> 00:05:04,079
After the review, Senjin could draft a Google Meet invite, show it to a person, send nothing until they say yes, then start the call at the right time and wait for the candidate.

61
00:05:04,079 --> 00:05:08,403
The invite would say an AI conducts and transcribes the interview.

62
00:05:08,403 --> 00:05:15,020
I have not tested any of it, and whether a candidate has to knock to enter is still an open question.

63
00:05:15,020 --> 00:05:16,527
Third, a clearer voice.

64
00:05:16,527 --> 00:05:21,507
I compared eight voices by ear, and Senjin now speaks twenty percent slower.

65
00:05:21,507 --> 00:05:27,797
Speech recognition mishearing names has been a bigger problem than the candidate hearing Senjin.

66
00:05:27,797 --> 00:05:32,055
Fourth, report upload to Drive or Slack, only when a person asks.

67
00:05:32,055 --> 00:05:35,987
It is not built, and sharing the file by hand needs no code.

68
00:05:36,487 --> 00:05:36,946
Limits.

69
00:05:36,946 --> 00:05:39,833
Speech recognition mishears names and terms.

70
00:05:39,833 --> 00:05:42,391
The voice is still obviously synthetic.

71
00:05:42,391 --> 00:05:46,656
Rules about automated interviews differ by place, so check yours.

72
00:05:46,656 --> 00:05:48,558
Three things I would pass on.

73
00:05:48,558 --> 00:05:52,560
One: let code run the process and keep the model to language.

74
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:56,890
Two: make every claim in a report point to evidence you can check.

75
00:05:56,890 --> 00:06:02,860
Three: read the actual transcripts, because the averages never showed me any of these bugs.

76
00:06:02,860 --> 00:06:05,222
The code and the docs are on GitHub.

